.TH SED 1
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
[
.B \-n
]
[
.B \-e
.I script
] ...
[
.I script
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sed
copies the named
.I files
(standard input default) to the standard output,
edited according to a script of commands.
Each
.B \-e
option adds its
.I script
to the whole.
If there is no
.B \-e
option, the first argument after the flags is the script.
The
.B \-n
option suppresses the default output.
.PP
A script consists of editing commands,
separated by newlines or semicolons,
of the following form:
.IP
[\fIaddress\fR [\fB,\fI address\fR] ] [\fB!\fR] \fIfunction\fR
.PP
In normal operation
.I sed
cyclically copies a line of input into a
.IR "pattern space" ,
applies in sequence
all commands whose
.I addresses
select that pattern space,
and at the end of the script copies the pattern space
to the standard output (except under
.BR \-n )
and deletes the pattern space.
.PP
An
.I address
is either a decimal number that counts
input lines, a
.B $
that
addresses the last line of input, or a context address,
.BI / regular-expression / \fR,
in the style of
.IR regexp (6).
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR sed ,
line numbers and
.B $
count within each file, not across all of them.
.PP
A command with no addresses selects every pattern space.
.PP
A command with
one address selects each pattern space that matches the address.
.PP
A command with
two addresses selects the inclusive range from the first
pattern space that matches the first address through
the next pattern space that matches
the second.
(If the second address is a number less than or equal
to the line number first selected, only one
line is selected.)
Thereafter the process is repeated, looking again for the
first address.
.PP
A
.B !
after the addresses applies the function to the pattern spaces
the addresses do not select.
.PP
The functions are:
.TP
.B d
Delete the pattern space.
Start the next cycle.
.TP
.B p
Print.
Copy the pattern space to the standard output.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
Copy the pattern space to the standard output (except under
.BR \-n )
and stop.
.TP
.BI s/ regular-expression / replacement / flags
Substitute the
.I replacement
string for instances of the
.I regular-expression
in the pattern space.
Any character may be used instead of
.BR / ,
and the delimiter appears in either part as
.BI \e delimiter\fR.
.I Flags
is zero or more of
.RS
.TP
.B g
Global.
Substitute for all non-overlapping instances of the
.I regular expression
rather than just the
first one.
.TP
.B p
Print the pattern space if a replacement was made.
.RE
.IP
An ampersand
.B &
appearing in the replacement
is replaced by the string matching the regular expression.
.B \en
in the replacement is a newline, and
.B \e
before any other character is that character.
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sed 10q file
Print the first 10 lines of the file.
.TP
.B sed '/^$/d'
Delete empty lines from standard input.
.TP
.B sed 's/UNIX/& system/g'
Replace every instance of
.B UNIX
by
.BR "UNIX system" .
.TP
.B sed -n '/^bind/p' file
Print only the lines that begin with
.BR bind .
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/sed/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grep (1),
.IR regexp (6)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty,
.B "can't open"
when a file could not be opened,
.B "bad script"
when the script does not parse, or
.BR usage .
The other files are still edited.
.SH BUGS
The functions above are all there are:
no hold space, no
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
.BR c ,
.BR y ,
.BR N ,
.BR w ,
.B r
or branches, and no
.B \-f
to read a script from a file.
Regular expressions have no captures, so
.B \e1
in a replacement is the character
.BR 1 .
A range that is open when one file ends carries on into the next.
